{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789380602,"nonce":"KQlLzViuprvpJxUFCAvQPBtf3HI=","title":"Scalar replacement of a loop-carried allocation (partial escape analysis)","message":"`bench_op_overload.py` after 7d0392d: twenty million `acc = acc + a`\nrun in 125 ms, 6 ns each, against PyPy's 88 to 133 ms for the whole\nprogram and ZynML's 72 ms with a value struct. Startup and compile add\n45 ms on top, so the run must come down to about 40 ms to win.\n\nPer iteration the loop now does, in one function after inlining:\n\n    x/y/z = load acc.*; load a.*; fadd x3\n    free(acc)\n    new = malloc 32; store tag; store 0.0 x3; store x/y/z\n    acc' = phi [new, body], [null, raised]\n\nEverything is in view and nothing escapes: `acc` and `new` are only\nread at constant offsets, compared with null, freed, or handed to the\nheader phi. PyPy removes the allocation by virtualising the object in\nthe trace; the HIR equivalent is scalar replacement across the phi.\n\nThe transform, for a header phi P of pointer type:\n\n1. Every incoming on a back edge is a malloc in the body (or null from\n   an error path); the entry incoming may be any pointer.\n2. Every use of P, of each back-edge malloc, and of every phi they flow\n   into is a gep at a constant offset feeding a load or store, a\n   ptrtoint compared with zero, a free, or a phi incoming.\n3. Replace P by one phi per field offset (and one bool phi for null-\n   ness when a null incoming exists). Loads read the field phi, stores\n   into the malloc define the next iteration's incoming, the malloc\n   and the frees disappear, the null compare reads the null-ness phi.\n4. The entry object: load its fields on the entry edge and free it\n   there if the phi owned it (drop_insert had placed its release in the\n   body). After the loop, reads of P use the field phis' exit values.\n\n`scalar_replace_alloc` does the single-block version of the same\nthing and has the offset and use classification to build on; the new\npart is the phi and the entry edge. Dead default stores (the\nconstructor's zero-init of x/y/z before __init__ stores them) fall out\nwith it, or separately from a per-block dead store elimination.\n\nExpected: the loop becomes three fadd on six phis, on the order of\nZynML's value-struct version.","files":null}]}